# Working environment

Copy this file to the directory the agent starts in (usually the read-write code bind) and
rename it `CLAUDE.md`, then fill in the mount table for your session. Claude Code loads
`CLAUDE.md` from the working directory and its parents, so it must live inside a bind — not
in the euler_agents repo, which is read-only and not an ancestor of the working directory.

---

You are running inside an Apptainer (Singularity) container on an ETH Euler compute node.
The container is the sandbox: permission prompts are bypassed, so the mount table below is
the actual boundary on what you can affect.

## Paths are the same inside and outside

Every bound directory is mounted **at its host path**. An absolute path you write into a
script, a SLURM job file, a shebang or a conda prefix is therefore valid on the host as well.
Do not invent translations; use the paths in the table as they are. The one exception is
`~`, which is `/home`, a private agent home — never the user's real home (see below).

## Mounts

Fill this in per session — it must match the `--extra-bind` / `--extra-read-bind` flags used
to start the container (the launcher prints each resolved bind as `Extra bind: SRC:DEST:MODE`,
and the host tool dirs as `Host ...: PATH (mode)`).

| Path | Mode | What it is |
|---|---|---|
| `/cluster/project/<group>/<user>/agentic_ai/workspaces/<project>` | read-write | The workspace and your starting directory, on project storage. Large outputs go here. |
| `/cluster/home/<user>/src/<repo>` | read-write | The code repository you are working on. Backed by the user's home, which has a quota: source only, no data. |
| `/cluster/project/<group>/<user>/<data>` | **read-only** | Input data. Writes will fail; that is intentional. |
| `/cluster/project/<group>/<user>/conda/envs` | read-only | The user's conda environments. Activate them; do not install into them. |
| `/cluster/project/<group>/<user>/conda/pkgs` | read-write | Shared conda package cache. |
| `/cluster/project/<group>/<user>/pixi` | read-write | `PIXI_HOME`: the `pixi` binary and its global config (envs and cache redirected here). |
| `/cluster/project/<group>/<user>/agentic_ai/cache` | read-write | `XDG_CACHE_HOME` and `CUDA_CACHE_PATH`: pip, uv, torch, Hugging Face and CUDA caches. |
| `/cluster/home/<user>/src/euler_agents` | read-only | The launcher scripts. Reference only. |
| `/home` | read-write | Agent state and credentials. Not the user's real home. |
| `/tmp` | read-write | Node-local scratch, discarded when the job ends. |

Nothing outside these paths exists inside the container. If a path you expect is missing, it
was not bound — say so rather than working around it.

## Things that differ from a normal shell

- **`~` is `/home`, a private agent home.** It is not the user's host home and (unless this
  is a Remote Control session) it is discarded when the job ends. Never put project files,
  environments or caches there; the cache variables above already point elsewhere.
- **A bound directory's parents look empty.** They exist only as mount points, so listing
  `/cluster/project` shows just the pieces that were bound. Do not conclude from this that
  files are missing or were deleted.
- **There is no SLURM.** `sbatch`, `squeue` and `srun` are not installed. You cannot submit
  or query jobs. If work needs a batch job or a GPU allocation, write the job script — with
  the real paths, which need no translation — and ask the user to submit it from outside.
- **No `sudo`.** `apt-get` exists but cannot install (no root). Use `conda`, `mamba`, `pixi`
  or `uv`.
- **No git credentials.** You can clone public repositories and commit in any read-write
  bind, with a preset identity and a `Co-Authored-By` trailer. You cannot push; the user
  reviews and pushes from the host after the run. Leave commits on a branch and say so.

## Available toolchain

Verified present in the image: `conda`, `mamba` and `uv` at `/opt/conda/bin`, Python 3.13,
`git` 2.43. `pixi` comes from the host through the `PIXI_HOME` bind when configured.
`CONDA_ENVS_DIRS` is `<workspace>/conda_envs:<host conda envs>:/opt/conda/envs`: create new
environments with plain `conda create -n NAME` and they land in the workspace at a path that
also exists on the host; the user's existing environments are visible read-only.

## Where to write what

- **Source code** → the read-write code bind. It is backed by the user's home directory, which
  has a quota — keep it to source, not data.
- **Large or generated output** (figures, tables, intermediates, conda envs) → the workspace,
  which is on project storage.
- **Scratch** → `/tmp`, and expect it to vanish.

Prefer passing input and output locations in as arguments or environment variables rather than
hardcoding them, so the same code runs against a different dataset without editing. When a
path must be absolute, the host path in the table is the correct one, inside and outside.
